{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558092-technomancers-and-banality","id":1558092,"title":"Technomancers and Banality","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-02-03T07:47:53-08:00","id":1558092,"title":"Technomancers and Banality","content":"While reading the<i> Changeling:The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition</i>, something in the section dedicated to the other splats of WoD, specifically mages, caught my attention (p. 351):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Modern cynics are a taste of what this age may have in<br /> store for all magi. Most believe in scientific advancement,<br /> but would rarely stoop to admit a belief in anything mysti-<br /> cal, fae included. Computers, machines, and occasionally<br /> magical fetishes and Treasures channel their Arts. <b>A cynic<br /> is capable of having his eyes opened to the broader spectrum<br /> of magic. If this happens, his Glamour is as refreshing as an<br /> icy stream. Until then, that stream is toxic to fae, afflicted<br /> with an average to high Banality.</b> </div> </div> </div>To me, the paragraph clearly points to technomancers, mainly to mages like the Etherites or the Virtual Adepts, not complety adhering to the Enlighneted Science of the Technocracy, but still using science (or science-adjacent) focuses/instruments in their practices. But, in Mage's terms, what's the text implying with &quot;<i>having his eyes opened to the broader spectrum of magic</i>&quot;? Could it refer to the increase of Arete and how mages can discard instruments after a given level of Arete? That would imply that technomancers would stop producing Banality after achieving Arete 6. Maybe, it's more of a question of paradigm, so to stop producing Banality, would the technomancer have to abandon paradigms like <i>Tech Holds All Answers</i>?<br /> <br /> I just remembered that in Revised Tradition Book for the Virtual Adepts (p. 38), it's written (in-character, so not a universal position) that they don't believe, from their experience, that the fae exist. It would make some sense, if they are sources of Banality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Fairies don\u2019t exist. Ghosts, on the other hand, do, but only<br /> on the Web. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","name":"Agrat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28986_1.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-03T07:52:25-08:00","id":1558093,"title":"","content":"Reads to me like the Traditions/Technocracy divide: a mage who adheres to the Technocratic paradigm as the only acceptable paradigm and rejects others, versus a mage who works through a technomantic paradigm but accepts the coexistence and validity of other paradigms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-03T10:24:05-08:00","id":1558096,"title":"","content":"To clarify, each type of mage is distinguished in this section in relation to the Glamour/Banality they source.<br /> <br /> For Marauders:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Marauders are drunk on the Dreaming. Completely consumed<br /> by Bedlam, they are whirlwinds of wild magic. A Marauder\u2019s<br /> magic is as unpredictable as a pooka on methamphetamine;<br /> a constantly moving storm of Glamour surrounds her, while<br /> her Banality is low to nonexistent. </div> </div> </div>For your typical Traditionalist/Craft mage, which isn't associated immediately with technomagick:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mystics are champions for the old ways, when magic was<br /> strong and the relationship between fae and magi was prac-<br /> tically familial. A mystic magus is half-enchanted \u2014 his<br /> Banality is low, Glamour rich, and his magics are true Arts<br /> in the most wonderful sense </div> </div> </div>For mages with a particular paradigm (I read as mainly paradigms or practices related to different religions) in which the Fae are antagonized:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> True believers claim to draw powers from demons, elementals,<br /> or gods. Their spells often involve blessings, hexes, elemental<br /> mastery, and harmful magic intended to punish those who<br /> go against their beliefs. A true believer recognizes fae as<br /> servants of unholy or unworthy powers. She glows with<br /> Glamour, but the mage\u2019s belief taints it, and can mentally<br /> affect anyone who saps that Glamour away\u200b </div> </div> </div>And, finally, Technocrats:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Technocrats know magic is just unexplored science, so they<br /> explore the wondrous heights and terrifying depths of phys-<br /> ics and chemistry to come up with cybernetics, weapons of<br /> mass destruction, and other high-tech tools. These magi are<br /> deeply banal and extremely dangerous. Few Technocrats<br /> believe in changelings; those who do want them wiped<br /> from the Earth, as they represent the old ways. Technocrats<br /> possess no Glamour. They carry pure Banality \u2014 capable of<br /> burning a changeling from the inside out\u200b </div> </div> </div>So, yeah, the &quot;cynics&quot; represented in the paragraph are almost certain not related to the Technocracy. Belief in the Arts that would empower a Changeling with Glamour would be, in their terms, highly unmutual. I <i>could</i> see the argument that a Technocract with a Primordial Eidolon, as they are described in <i>Guide to The Technocracy</i> (being the agents who search for forbidden knowledge beyond the confines of Enlightned Science) could join the former paragraph, but beyond that I think it's pure Banality all the way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","user":"Agrat","date":". 02-03-2026, 01:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","name":"Agrat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28986_1.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-03T11:24:48-08:00","id":1558097,"title":"","content":"As of <b>Technocracy Reloaded</b>, most Technocrats <i>do </i>believe in Magick. Or rather, they believe that Magick is real, dangerous, and fundamentally different from the Enlightened Science that they practice. They tend to have a similar view of the fae, but with more justification on the final point.<br /> <br /> Note that <b>C20</b> came out before <b>Technocracy Reloaded</b>. I would mostly disregard what <b>C20</b> says about Technocrats having Banality, with one exception \u2014 sort of. <b>Book of Secrets</b> mentions a new type of Marauder that's rooted in Denial instead of Madness; the Negation Men. <i>Those </i>guys strike me as being fundamentally Banal. And the nature of the Technocracy is such that I would expect Denial to be disproportionately common in their ranks, along with an infestation of Negation Men. The majority of Technocrats wouldn't be Negation Men and wouldn't cause Banality; but there's would be just enough to give the Technocracy a bad rep among changeling society.<br /> <br /> (Addendum: this is my go-to explanation for all claims involving the stereotypes found in earlier editions: Technocrats that don't believe in the existence of Magick are in Denial, and don't represent the typical Technocrat; but they stand out, and give other Technocrats a bad name.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 02-04-2026, 08:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-03T21:45:52-08:00","id":1558108,"title":"","content":"I think the best response to the Prodigals section of C20 is to just ignore it unless you're play C20 and only C20; and even then it needs to be taken with a few cups of salt.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, while C20 broadly does a lot to fix CtD (since it never got a Revised edition it was still very mired in early 90s messes), this section in 2e was poorly received by fans of crossover and C20 did far more repeating of past issues instead of doing better.<br /> <br /> The two main issues can be summed up fairly easily:<br /> <br /> 1) The Prodigals section is out of line with the C20 revisions to Banality to make Banality a more functional game element for play after 20 years of technological advancement and a player base that only grew more and more tired of the early WoD's &quot;science bad&quot; takes. C20 does a lot to make it clear that science and technology aren't the problem, but that they correlate to the problem in that they allow Banal systems to spread faster and more effectively. The idea of video games being a &quot;chore&quot; where you have to log on regularly to get your normal grind in as companies push for things that manipulate gamers into spending as much time (and thus money) in one game as possible is where things go &quot;bad.&quot; This leads the clustering of mages in C20 to be very weird as technomages like the Etherites who would look like very low Banality characters are treated as just below the Technocrats. As well as groups like the Hermetics, who are notorious for their desire to categorize, standardize, and classify magic over experimenting and doing crazy things with it get lumped in with the other mystics because their goals include a more magic friendly world despite their methods.<br /> <br /> 2) The Prodigals section of CtD has always been notorious for introducing lore contradictions that go far beyond expressing things from the changeling perspective or sacrifices in nuance for brevity since there's a lot to pack into a few pages. This goes beyond &quot;from a certain point of view&quot; things where we have examples where in C20 the Get of Fenris are all obsessed with an ancient blood feud with the Sidhe and anyone that hangs out with a Sidhe, while no WtA book presents the Get as having any particular animus towards any fae creatures. Considering how much C20 presents various other factions of the WoD completely differently from how they are in their own books - with a lot larger assumptions of crossover, exchange of occult knowledge, and general availability of access to each other - a lot of us just ignore these things completely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-04T16:48:11-07:00","id":1559222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Agrat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558092-technomancers-and-banality#post1558092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While reading the<i> Changeling:The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition</i>, something in the section dedicated to the other splats of WoD, specifically mages, caught my attention (p. 351):<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> To me, the paragraph clearly points to technomancers, mainly to mages like the Etherites or the Virtual Adepts, not complety adhering to the Enlighneted Science of the Technocracy, but still using science (or science-adjacent) focuses/instruments in their practices. But, in Mage's terms, what's the text implying with &quot;<i>having his eyes opened to the broader spectrum of magic</i>&quot;? Could it refer to the increase of Arete and how mages can discard instruments after a given level of Arete? That would imply that technomancers would stop producing Banality after achieving Arete 6. Maybe, it's more of a question of paradigm, so to stop producing Banality, would the technomancer have to abandon paradigms like <i>Tech Holds All Answers</i>?<br /> <br /> I just remembered that in Revised Tradition Book for the Virtual Adepts (p. 38), it's written (in-character, so not a universal position) that they don't believe, from their experience, that the fae exist. It would make some sense, if they are sources of Banality.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I don't remember where exactly I heard this, but I feel it should be stated:<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;A skeptic does not deny the existence of proof. A skeptic demands the existence of proof.&quot;</i> - what this means, is that a skeptic will claim there's no such thing as fairies, because the evidence points to that being the case. However, if you can provide them with some hard evidence that supports they do exist, then they will change their stance to reflect that.<br /> <br /> On another note, I believe that Technomancy would ironically serve to bolster the Dreaming, rather than diminish it.<br /> <br /> Right now, things like plasma swords, interstellar star ships, faster than light travel, teleportation, food replicators, est. all remains in the realm of science fiction. It's fantasy, imagination, stuff that couldn't exist in our reality.<br /> <br /> You take even a fraction of those ideas and start bringing them into reality, and it's gonna be like the moon landing all over again. A massive surge of glamour as humanity begins embracing new and exciting ideas for the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":29009,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","user":"DarkKing","date":". 04-04-2026, 07:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-08T06:36:26-07:00","id":1561466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558092-technomancers-and-banality?p=1559222#post1559222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't remember where exactly I heard this, but I feel it should be stated:<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;A skeptic does not deny the existence of proof. A skeptic demands the existence of proof.&quot;</i> - what this means, is that a skeptic will claim there's no such thing as fairies, because the evidence points to that being the case. However, if you can provide them with some hard evidence that supports they do exist, then they will change their stance to reflect that.<br /> <br /> On another note, I believe that Technomancy would ironically serve to bolster the Dreaming, rather than diminish it.<br /> <br /> Right now, things like plasma swords, interstellar star ships, faster than light travel, teleportation, food replicators, est. all remains in the realm of science fiction. It's fantasy, imagination, stuff that couldn't exist in our reality.<br /> <br /> You take even a fraction of those ideas and start bringing them into reality, and it's gonna be like the moon landing all over again. A massive surge of glamour as humanity begins embracing new and exciting ideas for the future.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like those are more Etherite coded than Technocracy coded. Why would the Technocracy want to make food replicators, for instance? It would weaken the Syndicate enormously. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26432-jagnestormskull","name":"JagneStormskull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26432_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-08T11:23:37-07:00","id":1561474,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JagneStormskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558092-technomancers-and-banality?p=1561466#post1561466\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I feel like those are more Etherite coded than Technocracy coded. Why would the Technocracy want to make food replicators, for instance? It would weaken the Syndicate enormously.</div> </div> </div> </div>We already invented technology that can recycle garbage into disposable water bottles.<br /> <br /> I personally would never use them, but it's interesting to know they exist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T09:56:08-07:00","id":1562188,"title":"","content":"Changeling's description of other splats is plain wrong for a number of reasons.<br /> <br /> But the first is that, even more than most other games, in CtD you have to remember they're not the same game and not really the same setting.<br /> <br /> The Mages from CtD are simpler, period: their Technocracy is a conspiracy of cynics trying to obliterate nonconformity, their Traditions are champions of literal old traditions, Paradigms and other complex Mage philosophical questions don't exist.<br /> <br /> It also drinks a lot still on antiquated views the other lines moved from. A strong tendency to conflate the past with fantasy and modernity with apathy. An equivocated linking between Glamour and Psychosis/Madness. All that compounding to what Heavy Arms said: you can take their stories about the Prodigals as examples of how ludicrous their stories can be, but their descriptions don't match the actual gamelines in any meaningful way.<br /> <br /> Your best approach is to ignore everything they say about how Banal other supernaturals are, reach your own understanding of Banality and Glamour, then make up your own reading of where the splats should sit for your game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T12:10:53-07:00","id":1562190,"title":"","content":"I think a big stumbling block for treating it as &quot;not the same game and not really the same setting,&quot; is that CtD starts name dropping setting details from the other games. CtD doesn't stop at Hyperscientists, Technomages, and Mystics. It brings up the Union, and different Traditions. And it does the same thing for other games. It's not like Vampire's &quot;lupines&quot; that may or may not be the Garou, or Werewolf's &quot;Namers&quot; that may or may not be mages. Those sorts of maybe it is, maybe it isn't approaches makes it much easier to treat things said more distinctly. If the Order of Hermes is a thing in CtD, but CtD never really defines a unique version of it... what is it if not the Mage thing?<br /> <br /> Hence &quot;ignore all that&quot; becomes the most reasonable option. CtD can have wizards that the ST builds for their CtD game, or you can wrangle crossover and get the actual Order of Hermes translated to mesh with a CtD game; not try to stumble around an extremely ill-defined CtD take on the Order that isn't the Mage take on the Order.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T13:03:11-07:00","id":1562193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1558092-technomancers-and-banality?p=1562190#post1562190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hence &quot;ignore all that&quot; becomes the most reasonable option. CtD can have wizards that the ST builds for their CtD game, or you can wrangle crossover and get the actual Order of Hermes translated to mesh with a CtD game; not try to stumble around an extremely ill-defined CtD take on the Order that isn't the Mage take on the Order.</div> </div> </div> </div>I sincerely think Changeling's takes are so outlandish that the problem isn't saying you should ignore it in crossovers, is not saying you should ignore them, period. I don't know many CtD players that give any real credit to their stories.<br /> <br /> That's why my idea is less that Changelings have skewed perspectives on the others, and more that they don't worry about being even remotely accurate. They have more contact with others than normal because they can stay out of sight easily and have no common sense, but their correct observations are freely mixed with any story they think to fit.<br /> <br /> For Vampire the past is an unsolvable mystery. For Werewolf it's oral tradition and all it entails. For Mage, knowable and malleable as all else.<br /> For Changeling is just one more story to tell.<br /> <br /> But regardless if you take it as a trait of the setting or not, the end result is the same: just ignore it as far as the reality of your game is concerned. And for Banality make whatever fits your Chronicle.<br /> <br /> Personally I wouldn't have any Mage with more than Banality 5, and that would already be one quite there, unless you decide that Resonance affects Banality, which may be a good call in some Chronicles.<br /> <br /> Without Resonance affecting it, a Mage with Banality 6 is an effective anomaly: either one of the rare Sleepwalkers, someone under magical Dissociation (2+ personalities, one may be that high Banality), or a few Quiets/Marauders, and I don't even think every Clarity should lead to Banality every time. Otherwise, despite Banality not causing a Magical Loss, it's a reasonable alarm. A Mage broken enough by Conditioning to reach this is about to Sleep again.<br /> <br /> Of course, I never heard of this happening. And that's how rare this should be.<br /> <br /> Resonance depends a lot, and a case may be made then that the guidelines in CtD actually reflects common Resonance found in situations where they find and identify Mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T16:00:21-07:00","id":1562197,"title":"","content":"I think there's a serious lack of back up in CtD to treat changeling society broadly as valuing a good story to the detriment having any coherent narrative about how the world around them actually works. All changelings have some Banality because they all have to live in reality a bit too (or I guess just speed run Bedlam). There's no way for changelings to have a functional society and deal with CtD specific issues like the Dreaming and the conflict between the Courts if they never worry about accuracy. They're not Marauder mages that actively force their personal views on a bubble around them so they can believe whatever they want to believe.<br /> <br /> This is especially true for Grumps... that's pretty much their core role in the game: to be the ones not lost in the fantasy of the Dreaming so someone's making sure stuff gets done based on accurate information not just whatever crazy stories are getting told around the Freehold for entertainment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-26T16:32:43-07:00","id":1562207,"title":"","content":"I agree with that. I take the ludicrous stories as an example of what circulates around, but they surely need someone to find Wonder in actual accurate intelligence tasks.<br /> <br /> Not sure if many of their stories are that harmful, but learning what the Prodigals tell should be on the table.<br /> <br /> For the time being, though, we'll only get that if doing it ourselves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}